            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book perfectly maps the New Testament's 'weeping and gnashing of teeth' to its Old Testament precedents, specifically Psalm 112:10, which connects seeing the righteous blessed with grief (weeping), gnashing of teeth (rage), and perishing. The common interpretation assumes the gnashing is a reaction of physical agony or remorse to hellfire, but every other Biblical use of teeth-gnashing (Psalms, Job, Lamentations, Acts) denotes hostile rage and hatred toward an enemy or the righteous. Luke 13:28 explicitly places the gnashing at the sight of the patriarchs in the kingdom, mirroring the visual sequence in Psalm 112:10. The book's interpretation is therefore significantly better supported by the scriptural data.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The 'gnashing of teeth' signifies the hostile rage and hatred of the wicked directed at the righteous, rather than an expression of physical agony or remorse.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"This rage is especially triggered when the wicked see the righteous in the kingdom from which they themselves are excluded.\",\n    \"Their grief, hatred, gnashing, and destruction follow the established pattern of the Psalms and Stephen's accusers.\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter extends this reading to argue that the wicked are ultimately annihilated ('melt away' into the vocabulary of the grave/Sheol) rather than subjected to eternal conscious torment.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"The gnashing of teeth is primarily an expression of extreme physical anguish, despair, and remorse in response to the agonizing torments of final judgment, such as the 'furnace of fire' or 'outer darkness.'\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"Gnashing of teeth signifies the agony and sorrow of the condemned reacting to their punishment.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"The counter claims gnashing is a reaction of agony and remorse to punishment, while the book claims it is a reaction of hostile rage and hatred directed at the righteous.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"The phrase is often paired with the 'furnace of fire' and 'outer darkness,' environments traditionally associated with intense pain and suffering.\",\n    \"The pairing with 'weeping' strongly suggests a context of profound sorrow and suffering rather than purely defiant anger.\",\n    \"Throughout Christian history, this has been the standard imagery for the conscious suffering of the damned.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Explains the consistent Biblical usage of 'gnashing of teeth' in the Old Testament and Acts, which universally denotes hostility and rage, not physical pain.\",\n    \"Accounts for the specific trigger in Luke 13:28 (seeing Abraham and the prophets in the kingdom), which flawlessly parallels the wicked seeing the blessed in Psalm 112:10.\",\n    \"Explains the presence of both 'weeping' (grief/sorrow over loss) and 'gnashing' (rage at the righteous) as distinct but simultaneous reactions, matching the dual verbs 'grieved' and 'gnash' in Psalm 112:10.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Fits seamlessly with traditional traditional-historical assumptions about the nature of post-mortem punishment and the 'furnace of fire.'\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Psalm 112:10\",\n    \"Luke 13:28\",\n    \"Acts 7:54\",\n    \"Psalm 37:12\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
